The reason `logger.LogDebug` doesn't output anything while `logger.LogInformation` does is because the default minimum log level is set to `Information`. Debug-level logs are filtered out unless you explicitly configure the logging system to include them.

### How to enable Debug logging in .NET 6 Console app

You need to set the minimum log level to `LogLevel.Debug` in your logging configuration. You can do this by calling `.SetMinimumLevel(LogLevel.Debug)` on the `ILoggingBuilder` inside your `AddLogging` call.

Here is the corrected version of your `Program.cs`:

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var serviceProvider = new ServiceCollection()
    .AddLogging(builder =>
    {
        builder.ClearProviders();
        builder.AddConsole();
        builder.AddDebug();
        builder.SetMinimumLevel(LogLevel.Debug);  // <-- Set minimum log level here
    })
    .BuildServiceProvider();

var logger = serviceProvider.GetService<ILogger<Program>>();

// Now this will show output
logger.LogDebug("hello world");
logger.LogInformation("something");
```

### Explanation

- By default, the minimum log level is `Information`, so `LogDebug` messages are ignored.
- `SetMinimumLevel(LogLevel.Debug)` tells the logger to include debug and higher severity messages.
- `AddConsole()` and `AddDebug()` add console and debug output providers respectively.

### Additional tips

- If you want to configure logging via `appsettings.json` or environment variables, you can do that too, but for a simple console app, configuring it programmatically as above is easiest.
- Make sure your console window is not filtering output (some IDEs or terminals might filter debug output).

---

