The issue you're encountering is due to **capturing the loop variable `i` by reference** in the lambda you pass to each thread. Since all threads share the same reference to `i`, and the loop continues to increment `i` before the threads run, the threads may see the value of `i` at a later point than you expect.

### Explanation

In your code:

```cpp
for(int i=0; i<n; i++ ){
    threads.emplace_back(std::thread([&](){ print_id(i); }));
}
```

The lambda `[&](){ print_id(i); }` captures `i` **by reference**. When the thread actually runs, the value of `i` might have changed (or even gone out of scope), so multiple threads can print the same value or unexpected values.

### How to fix it

Capture `i` **by value** inside the lambda, so each thread gets its own copy of the current `i`:

```cpp
for(int i=0; i<n; i++ ){
    threads.emplace_back(std::thread([i](){ print_id(i); }));
}
```

Alternatively, you can pass `i` as an argument to the thread function:

```cpp
for(int i=0; i<n; i++ ){
    threads.emplace_back(std::thread(print_id, i));
}
```

### Complete fixed example:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <list>
#include <thread>

void print_id(int id){
    printf("Hello from thread %d\n", id);
}

int main() {
    int n = 5;
    std::list<std::thread> threads;

    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        // Capture i by value in the lambda
        threads.emplace_back(std::thread([i]() { print_id(i); }));
    }

    for(auto& t : threads) {
        t.join();
    }

    return 0;
}
```

### Why does this happen?

- When you capture by reference (`[&]`), all threads share the same `i` variable.
- The loop increments `i` quickly, so by the time threads start executing, `i` may have changed.
- Capturing by value (`[i]`) makes a copy of `i` for each thread, preserving the intended value.

---

**Summary:** Always be careful with variable capture in lambdas used in threads. Capture loop variables by value to avoid race conditions and unexpected behavior.

The issue you're encountering is due to the fact that the lambda function captures the variable `i` by reference (`&`). Since the threads are launched asynchronously, the value of `i` may change before the lambda function is executed, leading to incorrect values being printed.

To fix this, you should capture `i` by value in the lambda function. This ensures that each thread gets its own copy of `i` at the time the thread is created. Here is the corrected code:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <list>
#include <thread>

void print_id(int id){
    printf("Hello from thread %d\n", id);
}

int main() {
    int n = 5;
    std::list<std::thread> threads = {};
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        threads.emplace_back(std::thread([i](){ print_id(i); }));
    }
    for(auto& t: threads){
        t.join();
    }
    return 0;
}
```

By capturing `i` by value (`[i]`), each thread will have its own copy of `i` with the correct value at the time of thread creation. This should give you the expected output:

```
Hello from thread 0
Hello from thread 1
Hello from thread 2
Hello from thread 3
Hello from thread 4
```

Note that the order of the output may still vary because the threads are running concurrently, but each thread will print the correct value.

