```

### Explanation
- The `text-4xl` class in Tailwind sets the font size to a larger value. You can adjust this to other sizes like `text-3xl`, `text-5xl`, etc., depending on your design requirements.
- Tailwind does not automatically apply large font sizes to heading tags because it encourages utility-first styling, where you explicitly define styles for each element.

### Debugging Tip
If you're unsure about the styles being applied to an element, you can inspect it in your browser's developer tools. This will show you the computed styles and help you identify if Tailwind's reset styles are overriding the default browser styles.

### Optional: Customize Default Styles
If you want to apply default styles to heading tags across your project, you can customize Tailwind's configuration. For example, you can extend the `theme` or use the `@layer base` directive in your CSS file to define global styles:

```css
@tailwind base;
@tailwind components;
@tailwind utilities;

@layer base {
  h1 {
    @apply text-4xl font-bold;
  }
  h2 {
    @apply text-3xl font-semibold;
  }
  h3 {
    @apply text-2xl font-medium;
  }
}
```

This approach ensures that all `<h1>`, `<h2>`, etc., tags have consistent styles across your project without needing to manually add utility classes every time.

### Conclusion
The behavior you're seeing is expected due to Tailwind's reset styles. By explicitly applying font size classes or customizing the default styles, you can achieve the desired appearance for your headings.

The issue you're encountering is related to the `file?.bytes` property being `null`. This happens because the `FilePicker` plugin does not automatically load the file's content into memory unless you explicitly request it by setting the `withData` parameter to `true` when calling `FilePicker.platform.pickFiles`.

